# Header / top app bar

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A header tells people whose home they are in and gives a way back to the start. Home Assistant draws its own top bar across every screen. Civica adds a header pattern for pages you write yourself, such as a help page or a setup guide.

This page owns the household header and the identity rules. [Service navigation](service-navigation.html) owns the list of destinations, the current-route marker and the small-screen menu.

This page covers:

- Header / top app bar
  - `<header>`
  - `ha-top-app-bar-fixed`
  - `ha-two-pane-top-app-bar-fixed`
  - Civica header pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

The Home Assistant top bar has a menu button or back button on the left, a title, and action buttons on the right. It can have a second row for tabs. Content scrolls underneath it, and a shadow appears under the bar once the page has scrolled.

The Civica header pattern is a household name, linked to the home page, with an optional task name beside it and an optional short list of destinations.

<h3 id="identity-and-navigation-have-separate-responsibilities">Identity and navigation have separate responsibilities</h3>

The header owns the household name and the optional image. [Service navigation](service-navigation.html) owns the list of destinations, the current-route marker and the small-screen menu. Add navigation to a header only when the page needs several stable destinations.

A clear household or business name is the default identity. A crest or logo is optional; a plain text name is a valid identity. See [Personalise your home](../get-started/personalisation.html). The Civica project logo belongs to project documentation only. A header on a page you write is not a redesign of Home Assistant’s own sign-in, account or setup screens, which keep their own authentication and routing.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-header"><code>&lt;header&gt;</code></h3>

The plain HTML element for introductory content. When it sits directly in the page body, it is the page’s banner, which people using a screen reader can jump to. Inside an article, section or similar element it is only a heading area and is not a banner. A page should have one banner. Home Assistant’s own app bar already is one, so a header you add to a page inside a dashboard should not be a second.

Use a header landmark, a named home link and labelled navigation. A dashboard author does not write this element in a card.

<h3 id="child-ha-top-app-bar-fixed"><code>ha-top-app-bar-fixed</code></h3>

The top bar Home Assistant fixes across the top of most screens. It has a title, a menu or back button, action buttons, and an optional second row. The title is cut short with an ellipsis when it does not fit, so keep titles short.

The Civica themes set three values for the top bar. The text is white in every theme and the same in light and dark modes. The background and border take the theme’s colour: #1D70B8 in Civica and Civica Blue, and a colour of their own in the other six themes.

Values in Civica and Civica Blue:

| Part | Theme setting | Value |
|---|---|---|
| Background | `app-header-background-color` | #1D70B8 (theme colour) |
| Text and icons | `app-header-text-color` | #FFFFFF |
| Bottom border | `app-header-border-bottom` | 4px solid, in the background colour |

The theme leaves the bar’s height at Home Assistant’s default. The yellow focus colour `ha-color-focus` (#FFDD00) and dark focus text `civica-focus-text` (#0B0C0C) are also set. Check the contrast of white text on the header colour in your own theme.

The theme cannot replace the bar, its title, the menu button or the action buttons. The group of action buttons is marked as a toolbar without a label, so test it with a screen reader. Card-mod styles cards, not this bar.

<h3 id="child-ha-two-pane-top-app-bar-fixed"><code>ha-two-pane-top-app-bar-fixed</code></h3>

A variant of the top bar with a side pane, used by screens that list items on the left and show details on the right. With the pane on, the title sits above the pane, which is 250 pixels wide by default. A line in `divider-color` (#CECECE in light mode, #50565A in dark mode) separates the pane from the content, and the pane and the content scroll separately. It has the same three theme colours and the same limits as the single-pane bar.

<h3 id="child-civica-header-pattern">Civica header pattern</h3>

A household name, a home link and, optionally, a task name and a short list of destinations. It is made for pages you write, not for Home Assistant’s own bar. The pattern uses the same colour as the top bar, white text, and a yellow focus highlight with dark text.

- The household name links to the genuine home page. It must never refresh the page, open a menu or run a device action.
- A plain text name is enough. If you add a logo, keep its proportions and place it on a plain surface. Give it a short text alternative, or an empty one when the name next to it says the same thing.
- A long name wraps instead of squashing the logo.
- The task name is supporting context. The page keeps its own main heading.
- Show the current destination with more than colour or an underline.
- Put a [skip link](skip-link.html) before the header. A fixed or sticky header must not cover the focused item when someone scrolls with the keyboard.
- Use the same name and place on every page.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A header gives one clear way home and the household’s name in the same place.
- The Civica theme colours the Home Assistant top bar without replacing it.
- The theme cannot change the bar’s title, buttons or height.
- A long title is cut short.
- A fixed bar can cover focused content at high zoom.
- A header you add inside a dashboard may become a second banner, so use one only on pages you write.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use a focused header with only the name and the task for a single journey, and add navigation only where it is needed. A constrained task can have fewer exits than a browsing dashboard. Keep the name short and the home link in the same place on every page.

Test long names, keyboard focus, account names, menu opening and wrapping at 320 CSS pixels, in light and dark modes and with at least two theme colours, using [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html). Keep text and icons visible on every header colour, including with yellow focus.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: use a header landmark and labelled navigation.
- 2.4.1 Bypass Blocks: a skip link comes before the header.
- 2.4.4 Link Purpose: name the home link for where it goes.
- 2.4.7 Focus Visible and 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured: a fixed bar must not hide the focused item.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: titles are cut short, so check them at 320 CSS pixels.
- 3.2.3 Consistent Navigation: keep the header in the same place.
- 1.4.3 and 1.4.11 Contrast: check the header colours, including the focus colours, in your own theme.

